The Core Rule

Present Perfect / Past Perfect + a specific past-time expression = ERROR
Specific past-time expressions ("yesterday," "last night," "an hour ago," "two years ago," "in 1990") always demand Simple Past.

Why: Present Perfect is for actions with no definite time — connecting the past to the present 
("I have finished my work" — we don't know exactly when, and it matters now). 
The moment you attach an exact time stamp, that connection to "now" breaks, and Simple Past is required instead.


Example-by-example
(a) ❌ He has left for London last night.

✅ He left for London last night.
"Last night" is a specific time → forces simple past, not "has left."

(b) ❌ She had seen the movie yesterday.

✅ She saw the movie yesterday.
"Yesterday" is a definite time → simple past, not past perfect.

(c) ❌ Two years ago, the earthquake had destroyed the city.

✅ Two years ago, the earthquake destroyed the city.
"Two years ago" is a specific past reference → simple past, not past perfect. (There's also no second past event to compare it to, which past perfect would require).
(d) ❌ I was hearing the dog barking last evening.

✅ I heard the dog barking last evening.
"Hear" is generally a non-continuous (stative) verb (perception verb), so "was hearing" is unnatural — plus "last evening" reinforces that simple past is correct.

(e) ❌ Newton has discovered that the force of gravitation makes apples fall.

✅ Newton discovered that the force of gravitation makes apples fall.
Historical facts/discoveries by a named person in the past always take simple past, not present perfect — even without an explicit time marker, "Newton discovered" is a completed, dated historical event.

(f) ❌ I have taken my lunch an hour ago.

✅ I took my lunch an hour ago.
"An hour ago" = specific time → simple past, not present perfect.


1. Present Perfect is correct with vague/unfinished time words

No time-marker conflict here — these are fine with present perfect:

I have finished my work already.
She has just left.
They have never been to Paris.
He has lived here since 2010 / for five years.


3. Past Perfect needs TWO past actions being compared

Past perfect isn't just "very old past" — it specifically shows which of two past events happened first.

By the time I reached the station, the train had left.
(Two actions, past perfect = the earlier one.)
